Q: How to ensure that closures created in a loop each capture their own copy of a loop variable without using the “let” keyword? A: <p>Beside of having a closure, you could take the rest of the parameter of <code>setTimeout</code> for parameters at calling time for the handed over function.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var codes = [1, 2, 3];

for (var i = 0; i &lt; codes.length; ++i) {
    setTimeout(function(j) { console.log(codes[j]); }, 100, i);
    //                                                      ^
    //                  ^                      ^
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Using node request or axios with streams to download and unpack file not handling back pressure as expected? A: <p>In Node.js, when you attach an event listener .on('data', ...) to a stream, you put that stream into continuous flow mode. The pipe() attempts to manage the backpressure, and your .on('data') listener continues to receive data as fast as the network can deliver it. Since the local network is much faster than the processor can decompress the file, the node begins to store the files in memory, waiting for zlib to process them, causing a memory overflow.</p>
<p>See what happens here to understand better <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20769132/whats-the-proper-way-to-handle-back-pressure-in-a-node-js-transform-stream">What&#39;s the proper way to handle back-pressure in a node.js Transform stream?</a></p>
<p>I recommend using got for this case, as it has a native API got.stream() and you don't need to access response.data.</p>
<p>Install with npm install got</p>
<p>Example of use for your case:</p>
<pre><code>const got = require('got');

const downloadWithGot = async (url, dest) =&gt; {
  const stream = got.stream(url, {
    username: 'user',
    password: 'pass',
    timeout: { request: 0 },

    retry: {
      limit: 3,
      methods: ['GET']
    }
  });

  const unzip = zlib.createUnzip({
    chunkSize: 64 * 1024 
  });

  const writer = fs.createWriteStream(dest, {
    highWaterMark: 64 * 1024 
  });

  let lastProgress = 0;
  stream.on('downloadProgress', ({ percent, transferred, total }) =&gt; {
    const progress = Math.floor(percent * 100);
    if (progress % 5 === 0 &amp;&amp; progress !== lastProgress) {
      lastProgress = progress;
      console.log(`progress: ${progress}%`);
      console.log(`bytes: ${(transferred / 1024 / 1024).toFixed(2)} MB`);
      if (total) {
        console.log(`Total: ${(total / 1024 / 1024).toFixed(2)} MB`);
      }
    }
  });

  await pipeline(stream, unzip, writer);
};
</code></pre>
<p>More here <a href="https://github.com/sindresorhus/got" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/sindresorhus/got</a></p>
<p>Using with stream <a href="https://github.com/sindresorhus/got/blob/main/documentation/3-streams.md" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/sindresorhus/got/blob/main/documentation/3-streams.md</a></p>
<p>--------------------------------------</p>
<p>You can also use Native HTTP asynchronously to resolve this case.</p>
<p>But you will need greater control for this case using https (for security) without using Axios.</p>
<p>Native HTTP will pause reading from the TCP socket when it detects backpressure management to slow down the rate, preventing data from accumulating in memory.</p>
<p>Sample:</p>
<pre><code>const https = require('https');

const downloadWithNative = (url, dest) =&gt; {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {
    const parsedUrl = new URL(url);

    const options = {
      hostname: parsedUrl.hostname,
      path: parsedUrl.pathname + parsedUrl.search,
      auth: 'user:pass',
      highWaterMark: 16 * 1024 // 16 KB
    };

    https.get(options, (response) =&gt; {
      if (response.statusCode !== 200) {
        reject(new Error(`Status: ${response.statusCode}`));
        return;
      }

      const unzip = zlib.createUnzip({
        chunkSize: 64 * 1024
      });

      const writer = fs.createWriteStream(dest, {
        highWaterMark: 64 * 1024
      });

      let isPaused = false;
      response.on('data', (chunk) =&gt; {
        const canContinue = unzip.write(chunk);
        if (!canContinue &amp;&amp; !isPaused) {
          response.pause();
          isPaused = true;
          unzip.once('drain', () =&gt; {
            response.resume();
            isPaused = false;
          });
        }
      });

      unzip.pipe(writer);

      writer.on('finish', resolve);
      writer.on('error', reject);
      response.on('error', reject);
      unzip.on('error', reject);
    });
  });
};
</code></pre>
<p>Here is another example of usage to understand native HTTP usage in Node: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/11944932/how-to-download-a-file-with-node-js-without-using-third-party-libraries">How to download a file with Node.js (without using third-party libraries)?</a></p>
